---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="End-to-End Contract Test for a Multi-Service AI System · San Lee"
  description="I traced one labeled note through an API, a classifier, a writeback, and a knowledge agent. The run checks the real schemas. It shows a consumer contract that dropped a field, and the check that closed the gap."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/the-system-run.html"
  ogType="article"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* One-note run plate (portfolio ADR-004). Page-scoped companion to the
       system diagram: a single note through notes-api, classifier, writeback,
       and kb-agent.

       The figure ladder in style.css now owns the scale, the ground, and the
       centring (ADR-014). The plate sits in a .fig .fig--plate wrapper, so
       `.fig--plate > svg` caps it at --fig-plate and centres it. This island
       DELETES its own width, max-width, and height declarations. It does not
       out-cascade them: a dead declaration reads as a live constraint to the
       next writer. Only the block margins stay page-scoped. */
    .run-plate {
      margin-top: 0;
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }
    .run-plate .rn-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .run-plate .rn-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    /* 9px, not 8px: the microtext floor (CLAUDE.md, ADR-014 section 5). The
       plate renders at scale 1.046 in a 320px viewport, so a declared 9px
       renders at 9.42px and clears the floor. */
    .run-plate .rn-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }
    .run-plate .rn-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* This island DELETED .rn-drop, .rn-drop-box, .rn-drop-text and the
       rn-head-drop marker fill. They drew a dashed oxide branch for a field
       the writeback did not map. notes-api mapped `region` on 2026-07-18, so
       the branch drew a state that no longer exists. --accent-warm means a
       reversal on this site, and a field that now lands is not a reversal.
       The plate shows the run as it is; step 3 keeps the account of the drop. */
    .run-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    /* The bare plate's caption. It moves to the consolidated grammar
       (ADR-014). It states what the plate argues, so it takes the what-voice.
       The values below are the ones `:is(.fig, figure[data-fig]) .fig-what`
       gives the caption further down this page. The class stays page-scoped for its VOICE only. The measure and the
       centring left it: style.css now reaches a bare plate's caption
       paragraph directly, through `.fig--plate > p`. */
    .run-plate-caption {
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      line-height: 1.55;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
    }

    /* This lane DELETED the two-slot caption block that used to sit here. The
       block was a copy of context-architecture.astro's rules at commit 9d81caa
       (pull request #247). That page took the copy because a concurrent
       session then held public/assets/style.css. The session merged, and
       ADR-014 gave the caption grammar one home. Three rules in style.css
       replace the copy: `.fig figcaption`,
       `:is(.fig, figure[data-fig]) .fig-what`, and
       `:is(.fig, figure[data-fig]) .fig-limit`. */

    /* tsr1-hygiene (ADR-013). The prefix carries a digit, because a marker id
       is global to the document and a duplicate id repaints the wrong arrow
       head where no gate sees it. The rn- prefix above is the only other
       prefix on this page.

       Two hues only. --accent marks the guard, which is what this page now
       reports. --accent-warm means a reversal on this site, and a guard that
       landed as planned is not a reversal, so it does not appear here.

       Every state is dual-encoded by a word and a shape. The path that runs is
       a solid box and it reads "nothing breaks". The guard is a solid box too,
       and the word BUILT carries its state. Colour marks salience here, and it
       never carries a state on its own. contrast-check.cjs exempts SVG text
       and no gate reads a plate, so the word half is the half that always
       survives.

       The figure ladder in style.css owns the scale and the centring
       (ADR-014): `figure[data-fig="plate"] > svg` caps the plate at
       --fig-plate and centres it. This island DELETES its width, max-width,
       and height declarations, for the same reason as .run-plate's. The plate
       sits inside .example-run, which sets padding 0 and list-style none, so a
       list item is the full content track: 272px on a 320px phone. */
    .tsr1-hygiene {
      margin-top: 14px;
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }
    .tsr1-hygiene .tsr1-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .tsr1-hygiene .tsr1-title,
    .tsr1-hygiene .tsr1-guard-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    /* 9px, not 8px: the microtext floor (CLAUDE.md, ADR-014 section 5). This
       plate renders at scale 1.046 in a 320px viewport, so a declared 9px
       renders at 9.42px and clears the floor. */
    .tsr1-hygiene .tsr1-micro {
      fill: var(--text-secondary);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }
    .tsr1-hygiene .tsr1-kicker {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 9px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }
    .tsr1-hygiene .tsr1-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* The guard that IS built: a solid accent outline, and the word BUILT
       beside it. The word is what carries the state; the accent stroke only
       marks the row this page reports. It is unfilled and carries no role,
       tabindex or handler, so the fill: none rule in CLAUDE.md does not bind
       it. */
    .tsr1-hygiene .tsr1-guard {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .tsr1-hygiene .tsr1-guard-mark {
      fill: var(--accent);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 9px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }
    .tsr1-hygiene marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav current="work" />

  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="the-system.html">&larr; Back to The System</a>
    <h1>One Note, End to End</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">One gold note through the real schemas. The writeback maps all three labels. It dropped one of them until a contract check found the difference.</p>
    <p class="ex-caption">
      The article text and all three predicted labels are real. They come from
      gold row <code>g002</code> in the classifier's eval set. That prediction
      matches the hand label and the LLM judge on every axis. The note's title,
      <code>id</code>, and timestamps are illustrative placeholders.
    </p>

    <!-- Run companion to the-system diagram: one note through the four seams.
         The plate drew a dashed oxide branch for a dropped `region` field until
         notes-api mapped it on 2026-07-18. It now draws three fields in and
         three tags out, which is what the writeback does. Step 3 keeps the
         account of the drop, so the incident moved rather than went away.
         The ordered list below still carries the five steps and the real
         payloads. ADR-010 is not applicable. There are no interactive nodes.

         The wrapper is a div with .fig .fig--plate, and it is deliberately not
         a figure element. A figure element in the content region must carry
         data-fig and both caption slots, and this plate's caption is one
         what-statement. The div joins the figure ladder and the lifted night
         ground, and it claims no contract that it does not meet (ADR-013,
         ADR-014). data-place="spine" pins the plate to the content track,
         which is the per-kind default for a plate. -->
    <div class="fig fig--plate" data-place="spine">
      <span class="fig-label">Plate 01</span>
      <svg class="run-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 84" role="img"
           aria-label="One note end to end. notes-api creates the note. The classifier returns three labels. The writeback maps all three as namespaced tags. kb-agent can then search by tag.">
        <title>One note end to end. notes-api creates the note. The classifier returns three labels. The writeback maps all three as namespaced tags. kb-agent can then search by tag.</title>
        <defs>
          <marker id="rn-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                  markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <!-- Four stage boxes L→R. rx matches --radius-sm (2px). -->
        <rect class="rn-box" x="2" y="8" width="56" height="40" rx="2" />
        <text class="rn-title" x="30" y="26" text-anchor="middle">notes-api</text>
        <text class="rn-micro" x="30" y="38" text-anchor="middle">POST</text>

        <rect class="rn-box" x="68" y="8" width="56" height="40" rx="2" />
        <text class="rn-title" x="96" y="26" text-anchor="middle">classifier</text>
        <text class="rn-micro" x="96" y="38" text-anchor="middle">3 fields</text>

        <rect class="rn-box" x="134" y="8" width="56" height="40" rx="2" />
        <text class="rn-title" x="162" y="26" text-anchor="middle">writeback</text>
        <text class="rn-micro" x="162" y="38" text-anchor="middle">3 tags</text>

        <rect class="rn-box" x="200" y="8" width="56" height="40" rx="2" />
        <text class="rn-title" x="228" y="26" text-anchor="middle">kb-agent</text>
        <text class="rn-micro" x="228" y="38" text-anchor="middle">search</text>

        <!-- Three L→R muted edges — open pipeline through the run. -->
        <path class="rn-edge" d="M 58 28 L 66 28" marker-end="url(#rn-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
        <path class="rn-edge" d="M 124 28 L 132 28" marker-end="url(#rn-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
        <path class="rn-edge" d="M 190 28 L 198 28" marker-end="url(#rn-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />

        <!-- The three fields travel one response over one edge, so the plate
             names them on one line. It does not draw region as a separate
             path: that would show a second seam that the system does not have. -->
        <text class="rn-micro" x="130" y="62" text-anchor="middle">cat + domain + region</text>
        <text class="rn-micro" x="130" y="78" text-anchor="middle">same note, real schemas</text>
      </svg>
      <p class="run-plate-caption">
        Create, classify, write three tags, search. The writeback maps every
        field the classifier returns. Step&nbsp;3 below records the one it
        dropped, and the check that closed the gap.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="example-run">
      <li>
        <p class="ex-step">A client creates a note</p>
        <p class="ex-note"><code>POST /notes</code> takes a title and content. It
          returns <code>201</code> immediately and schedules the enrichment as a
          background task, so <code>tags</code> is still empty at this point.</p>
        <pre><code>POST /notes
&#123;
  "title": "USS Ronald Reagan first ordnance drop",
  "content": "F/A-18E Hornets assigned to the \"Eagles\" of Strike Fighter Attack Squadron 115 ... in support of Operation Iraqi Freedom."
&#125;</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="ex-step">A forced tool call keeps the classifier in schema</p>
        <p class="ex-note">The background task POSTs the text to the classifier's
          <code>/classify</code> endpoint. One <code>claude-sonnet-5</code> call
          pins <code>tool_choice</code> to the <code>classify_article</code>
          tool. That forces the model to answer only as an enum-validated tool
          call. The endpoint returns the call as
          <code>&#123;category, operational_domain, region&#125;</code>. There is
          no confidence score, by design.</p>
        <pre><code>// forced tool_use emitted by the model
&#123;
  "name": "classify_article",
  "input": &#123;
    "category": "operations",
    "operational_domain": "air",
    "region": "middle-east"
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="ex-step">Labels become namespaced tags</p>
        <p class="ex-note">notes-api owns the writeback. It maps the fields it
          knows to prefixed tags. Note the <code>operational_domain</code>
          &rarr; <code>domain:</code> rename. It applies the tags with replace
          semantics, so a second run converges and writes no duplicate. It keeps
          the user's own tags.</p>
        <p class="ex-note">The background task writes through the ORM
          in-process, and it does not call the service's own HTTP endpoint. The
          equivalent <code>PUT /notes/&#123;id&#125;/tags</code> exists for
          external callers, and it carries the same replace semantics.</p>
        <p class="ex-note"><strong>This step dropped <code>region</code>, and
          that is what the audit found.</strong> The classifier grew a third
          field in v3. This consumer read two fields, and it ignored the rest.
          It did not break, because the writeback pulls the fields out by name
          and does not assume the response shape. A drop that breaks nothing is
          the drop that no build reports.</p>
        <p class="ex-note">The frozen contract for this seam requires a provider
          change and a coordinated consumer update together. The classifier
          shipped <code>region</code>, and that update did not land with it. No
          build went red, because each repo's &ldquo;contract test&rdquo;
          asserted against its own copy of the shape rather than a shared one.
          Nothing broke here, and nothing would have noticed if it had. The
          <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/architecture/blob/main/decisions/SYS-004-classify-http-contract.md">SYS-004 amendment</a>
          records it.</p>
        <p class="ex-note"><strong>That gap is closed.</strong>
          <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/architecture/blob/main/decisions/SYS-018-provider-owned-contract-artifacts.md">SYS-018</a>
          landed on 2026-07-18, and the same change maps <code>region</code>.
          The writeback writes three tags now, and the payloads on this page
          are the current ones. The classifier now publishes
          <code>contracts/classify-response.schema.json</code>, generated from
          its live response model, and this consumer fetches that file in CI
          and fails when its own field set diverges. A fetch failure still
          warns and exits 0, so a provider outage reads as a pass.
          <a href="the-system.html#decision-contracts">The full decision
          &rarr;</a></p>

        <!-- tsr1-hygiene (ADR-013). The page's own point: this consumer
             tolerates an unknown field, tolerance is not a guard, so somebody
             built the guard. Row two used to read NOT BUILT, which contradicted
             the note directly above it once SYS-018 landed. It now reads BUILT
             and names the one case the check does not catch.
             .run-plate above already shows the drop, so this
             plate does not repeat it. The seam that let the drift through is
             shown on the-system.html, and .fig-limit links there rather than
             showing it a second time in a second grammar. Zero digits here.
             Every count stays in the notes above.

             data-place="spine" here is DECLARATIVE and it paints nothing. The
             rules that read data-place are direct-child selectors, and this
             figure sits inside a list item. The attribute states the per-kind
             width policy for a plate, so a later move of this figure to the
             article grid keeps the policy it already carries. -->
        <figure class="fig" data-fig="plate" data-place="spine">
          <span class="fig-label">Plate 02</span>
          <svg class="tsr1-hygiene" viewBox="0 0 260 104" role="img"
               aria-label="Two rows. In the first, the consumer reads the fields it knows by name, so a new field costs it nothing and nothing breaks. That is hygiene. In the second, a check now fails the build when the two shapes drift apart. It does not fail on a fetch failure.">
            <title>Two rows. In the first, the consumer reads the fields it knows by name, so a new field costs it nothing and nothing breaks. That is hygiene. In the second, a check now fails the build when the two shapes drift apart. It does not fail on a fetch failure.</title>
            <defs>
              <marker id="tsr1-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                      markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
                <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
              </marker>
            </defs>

            <!-- The path that runs. Solid boxes, muted edges. rx matches
                 --radius-sm (2px), the same as .run-plate above. -->
            <text class="tsr1-kicker" x="2" y="9">Hygiene</text>

            <rect class="tsr1-box" x="2" y="14" width="66" height="30" rx="2" />
            <text class="tsr1-title" x="35" y="28" text-anchor="middle">a new field</text>
            <text class="tsr1-micro" x="35" y="39" text-anchor="middle">provider</text>

            <rect class="tsr1-box" x="85" y="14" width="72" height="30" rx="2" />
            <text class="tsr1-title" x="121" y="28" text-anchor="middle">read by name</text>
            <text class="tsr1-micro" x="121" y="39" text-anchor="middle">consumer</text>

            <rect class="tsr1-box" x="174" y="14" width="84" height="30" rx="2" />
            <text class="tsr1-title" x="216" y="28" text-anchor="middle">nothing breaks</text>
            <text class="tsr1-micro" x="216" y="39" text-anchor="middle">result</text>

            <path class="tsr1-edge" d="M 70 29 L 84 29" marker-end="url(#tsr1-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
            <path class="tsr1-edge" d="M 159 29 L 173 29" marker-end="url(#tsr1-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />

            <!-- The guard that SYS-018 built. The word BUILT carries the state,
                 so colour never carries it alone. The second line names the one
                 case the check does not catch, so the row does not overstate
                 when a reader takes it on its own. -->
            <text class="tsr1-kicker" x="2" y="59">A guard</text>

            <rect class="tsr1-guard" x="2" y="64" width="256" height="34" rx="2" />
            <text class="tsr1-guard-title" x="12" y="78">a check that fails the build</text>
            <text class="tsr1-guard-mark" x="248" y="78" text-anchor="end">Built</text>
            <text class="tsr1-micro" x="12" y="90">it fails on drift, not on a fetch failure</text>
          </svg>
          <figcaption>
            <span class="fig-what">Tolerance of an unknown field is consumer
              hygiene, not a guard. The writeback reads the fields it knows by
              name, so a new field costs it nothing. A shared check now fails
              the build when the two shapes drift apart. A fetch failure still
              exits 0, so a provider outage reads as a pass.</span>
            <span class="fig-limit">It does not show the seam that let the drift
              through. Each repository asserted against its own copy of the
              shape, and <a href="the-system.html">The System</a> shows that
              seam. The notes above keep the reasoning and the record
              link.</span>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <pre><code>classifier_tags(&#123;
    "category": "operations",
    "operational_domain": "air",
    "region": "middle-east",
&#125;)
# -&gt; ["category:operations", "domain:air", "region:middle-east"]

# written in-process; note.tags = merge_tags(note.tags, new_tags)
&#123; "tags": ["category:operations", "domain:air", "region:middle-east"] &#125;</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="ex-step">The note is now enriched</p>
        <p class="ex-note">A <code>GET</code> returns the labels in its flat
          <code>tags</code> list, and it returns <code>enrichment_status</code>
          beside them. The status starts at <code>pending</code>. The background
          task sets it to <code>done</code> after a writeback, and to
          <code>failed</code> after a classifier error. It stays
          <code>pending</code> when <code>CLASSIFIER_URL</code> is unset, so a
          local run never reads as a failure. The tags show the labels, and the
          status shows whether the task that wrote them finished.</p>
        <pre><code>GET /notes/1
&#123;
  "id": 1,
  "title": "USS Ronald Reagan first ordnance drop",
  "content": "F/A-18E Hornets ... Operation Iraqi Freedom.",
  "tags": ["category:operations", "domain:air", "region:middle-east"],
  "enrichment_status": "done",
  "published_at": null,
  "created_at": "...",
  "updated_at": "..."
&#125;</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="ex-step">The agent can find it</p>
        <p class="ex-note">The kb-agent's <code>search_notes</code> tool filters by
          tag over the same HTTP seam, so the freshly-tagged note is now retrievable
          for grounding. The result follows the system-wide observation contract
          (SYS-003): a status, a summary, the payload, and a cited source.</p>
        <pre><code>search_notes(tag="domain:air")  -&gt;  GET /notes?tag=domain:air
&#123;
  "status": "success",
  "summary": "1 matching note(s).",
  "payload": [
    &#123;
      "id": 1,
      "title": "USS Ronald Reagan first ordnance drop",
      "tags": ["category:operations", "domain:air", "region:middle-east"]
    &#125;
  ],
  "source": "notes-api service, http://127.0.0.1:8000/notes"
&#125;</code></pre>
      </li>
    </ol>

  </main>
  <script is:inline src="../assets/theme.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="../assets/last-updated.js"></script>
</Base>
